course: dictionary
lesson: 9620
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «The bird was struggling in the net» tells us the bird was fighting to get free, because it was caught. Here struggling means twisting and fighting, not trying hard at school.
explain: «I'm struggling with this sum» is different. Nobody is caught in a net. The speaker finds the sum difficult and needs help. Ask yourself what is happening in the sentence, then choose the meaning that fits.
explain: Struggling can also describe a thing or a group: «a struggling business» is one that is having a hard time. The word ends in -ing, but it is not always an action you can see. Check the clues around it.
fact: Struggling is the -ing form of the verb struggle.
fact: Struggling means trying very hard to do something difficult.
fact: Struggling also means fighting or twisting your body to get free.
fact: As an adjective, «struggling» describes a person or thing having a hard time, such as «a struggling team».
fact: In water, someone who is struggling needs help: shout for an adult or lifeguard, throw something that floats, and do not jump in yourself.
exercise: «Children who cook, mind siblings or look after a struggling parent are called young carers.» In this sentence, what does struggling mean? | *Having a hard time | Jumping over a fence | Singing loudly | Sleeping soundly
exercise: «The bird was struggling in the net.» What is the bird doing? | Flying very high | *Fighting and twisting to get free | Sitting still and resting | Eating its breakfast
exercise: Nadia says, «I'm struggling with this maths question.» What does she mean? | She is singing a song | *She is trying hard, and finds the question difficult | She is caught in a net | She has finished and is pleased
test: «Some say lower taxes help businesses create jobs; others say more public spending helps those struggling most.» Who are those struggling most? | People who are winning prizes | People who are singing at the top of their voices | People who are fast asleep | *People who are having a hard time
test: Someone is struggling in water and you want to help. What should you do first? | Laugh and walk away | *Shout for an adult or lifeguard, and throw something that floats | Jump straight in without a plan | Run off to find a snack
exam: What does struggling mean in «a struggling team»? | Glowing brightly | Winning a prize | *Having a hard time | Telling a joke
